# Email and password

Email and password give a player a permanent account across devices. On this page you will find the sign-up, sign-in, guest-to-permanent upgrade, password change, and reset flows, with request and response examples and SDK calls. All calls follow the [shared rules](overview.md#rules-that-apply-to-every-call) (environment header, CSRF).

## Rules

- The **email** is trimmed and lowercased; it must contain exactly one `@` and a dot in the domain part. An address can belong to only one account in the same environment.
- The **password** is 8–128 characters (counted in Unicode characters, not bytes). The password is stored with Argon2id.
- The **display name** is required; use 1–100 characters (the server enforces this limit on guest upgrade). The player can later change their name under the 2–24 character rule ([Guest sign-in › Display name](guest.md#display-name)).
- Today there is **no email verification endpoint**; sign-up returns `email_verified` as `false`.
- The SDKs do a plain bounds check before sending the request (email 3–320, password at most 1024, display name at most 256 units; no control characters). The real policy is on the server.

## Sign-up

```http
POST /v1/auth/sign-up/email
content-type: application/json
x-crx-env: <environment-id>
x-csrf-token: <csrf-token>

{"email":"player@example.com","password":"<at least 8 characters>","display_name":"Alex"}
```

`201` and the [shared sign-in envelope](overview.md#successful-sign-in-response) (`is_new_player: true`, `player.is_guest: false`, `player.email` filled) + session cookies.

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `400` | Email format or password policy violation |
| `409` | The address is registered in this environment (the body does not repeat the address) |
| `429` | 10 / 60 s per IP or 5 / 15 min per address exceeded |

## Sign-in

```http
POST /v1/auth/sign-in/email
content-type: application/json
x-crx-env: <environment-id>
x-csrf-token: <csrf-token>

{"email":"player@example.com","password":"<password>"}
```

`200` and the sign-in envelope (`is_new_player: false`).

- **A wrong password and an unknown address get the same response** (plain-text `403`); which one it was does not leak.
- An account with the **correct password but banned** gets `403` and `{"code":"account_banned","message":"account banned"}`; the cookie is not deleted.
- 10 failed sign-ins in 15 minutes for the same address + IP lock the account; while locked, even the correct password gets `429`. A successful sign-in refunds the allowance. There is also a separate 5 / 60 s limit per IP.
- If the request carries a usable session of another player (for example an old guest in the same browser), **that session family is revoked** after the sign-in succeeds. The same player's session is not touched.

## Guest upgrade

Turns a guest player into a permanent account with the same `player.id`; progress is kept. A guest **session** is required (a cookie or `Authorization: Bearer <session-token>`).

```http
POST /v1/auth/guest/upgrade/email
content-type: application/json
x-crx-env: <environment-id>
x-csrf-token: <csrf-token>

{"email":"player@example.com","password":"<password>","display_name":"Alex"}
```

`200`; the same `player.id`, `is_guest: false`, only a **new** session pair. The upgrade is a single database transaction: the identity is added, the guest secret is retired, old sessions are revoked, and a new session is opened.

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `400` | Email, password, or name validation |
| `401` | Environment header or session missing |
| `403` | The session is not active, belongs to another environment, or a CSRF rejection |
| `409` | The address is registered to another account in this environment; the operation is rolled back and the guest session is not damaged |
| `422` | The player is no longer a guest (a duplicate or concurrent request) |
| `429` | IP 10 / 60 s, guest player 30 / 60 s, or address 5 / 15 min exceeded |

If the response is lost (the connection dropped), the old guest sessions may have been revoked; the recovery path is a normal email sign-in.

## Password change

A session is required. The old password is verified; on success **all sessions** (including this one) are revoked and the cookies are deleted; the player signs in again.

```http
POST /v1/auth/change-password
content-type: application/json
x-crx-env: <environment-id>
x-csrf-token: <csrf-token>

{"old_password":"<current>","new_password":"<new>"}
```

`204`. Errors: `400` new password policy violation, `403` session or old password rejected, `429` IP 10 / 60 s or account 6 / 60 s. A wrong current password also counts toward the sign-in lock counter.

## Password reset

1. A reset email is requested with `POST /v1/auth/forgot-password`. **Whether or not the account exists, the response is the same `200`** (account existence does not leak).
2. `POST /v1/auth/reset-password` is called with the token from the email. The token is **single-use** and valid for **1 hour**. On success **all sessions are revoked**.

```http
POST /v1/auth/forgot-password
content-type: application/json
x-crx-env: <environment-id>
x-csrf-token: <csrf-token>

{"email":"player@example.com"}
```

```http
POST /v1/auth/reset-password
content-type: application/json
x-crx-env: <environment-id>
x-csrf-token: <csrf-token>

{"token":"<64 hex>","new_password":"<new>"}
```

`reset-password` errors: `400` new password policy violation (**the token is not burned**, you can retry with the same link), `403` token invalid/used/expired or CSRF (the distinction does not leak), `429` limit exceeded (5 / 60 s, the IP bucket shared with forgot and magic link).

> [!WARNING]
> **Known limitation:** The link in the reset email goes not to your game's page but to `/v1/auth/reset-password?token=…` at the server's API root. There is no `GET` endpoint on this path; that is, clicking the link does not show a page. To offer reset in your game, the player must carry the `token` value from the email to your own reset screen (for example by routing this path to a game page on your own reverse proxy; this routing is not verified in this repository). Email delivery depends on the operator's SMTP setting.

## SDK

<!-- snippet: examples/ts/auth-email.ts -->
```ts
import { CrxError, ErrorKind, type PlayerClient } from "@ferdiunal/open-play-ts";

/** Yeni hesap açar ve oturumu başlatır. Adres kayıtlıysa sunucu 409 döner (`ErrorKind.Http`, status 409). */
export async function register(client: PlayerClient, email: string, password: string, displayName: string): Promise<void> {
  await client.session.signUpEmail({ email, password, displayName });
}

/** Mevcut hesapla girer. Yanlış parola ile bilinmeyen adres aynı yanıtı alır; hangisi olduğu sızmaz. */
export async function login(client: PlayerClient, email: string, password: string): Promise<void> {
  await client.session.signInEmail({ email, password });
}

/**
 * Misafir ilerlemesini koruyarak kalıcı hesaba geçer: aynı `playerId`, yeni oturum.
 * Önce misafir olarak girilmiş olmalıdır; adres başka bir hesapta kayıtlıysa 409 döner ve misafir oturumu bozulmaz.
 */
export async function upgradeGuestToEmail(client: PlayerClient, email: string, password: string, displayName: string): Promise<void> {
  if (client.session.state.status !== "authenticated") await client.session.signInGuest();
  try {
    await client.session.upgradeGuest({ email, password, displayName });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof CrxError && error.kind === ErrorKind.Http && error.status === 409) {
      throw new Error("Bu e-posta adresi başka bir hesapta kayıtlı.");
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

/** Parola sıfırlama isteği: hesap olsa da olmasa da yanıt aynıdır. */
export async function requestReset(client: PlayerClient, email: string): Promise<void> {
  await client.session.requestPasswordReset({ email });
}

/** E-postadaki token ile yeni parola belirler; başarıda tüm oturumlar kapanır, oyuncu yeniden giriş yapar. */
export async function finishReset(client: PlayerClient, token: string, newPassword: string): Promise<void> {
  await client.session.resetPassword({ token, newPassword });
}

/** Oturum açıkken parola değiştirir; başarıda tüm oturumlar (bu dahil) kapanır. */
export async function changePassword(client: PlayerClient, currentPassword: string, newPassword: string): Promise<void> {
  await client.session.changePassword({ currentPassword, newPassword });
}
```

| TypeScript | Unity | Endpoint |
|---|---|---|
| `signUpEmail` | `SignUpEmailAsync` | `sign-up/email` |
| `signInEmail` | `SignInEmailAsync` | `sign-in/email` |
| `upgradeGuest` | `UpgradeGuestAsync` | `guest/upgrade/email` |
| `changePassword({ currentPassword, newPassword })` | `ChangePasswordAsync` | `change-password` (`old_password` in the body) |
| `requestPasswordReset` | `RequestPasswordResetAsync` | `forgot-password` |
| `resetPassword` | `ResetPasswordAsync` | `reset-password` |

Shared SDK behavior:

- The password and token are written only to the request body; they do not enter session state, event listeners, error objects, or logs.
- On the auth endpoints, only if the CSRF layer rejected a `403` (there is no new `x-csrf-token` in the response) does the SDK retry **once** with a fresh token.
- If the connection is lost after the request was sent, the sign-in endpoints make at most one session probe; if the outcome is uncertain, you get an `UnknownOutcome` error. Calls that return no result, such as password change and reset, are also not blindly retried.
- `upgradeGuest` sends no network request when there is no session; the server's definitive rejections (4xx) do not drop the guest session.
- On success, `changePassword` applies the same transition as `logout` (the state becomes `signedOut`).

Unity example:

<!-- snippet: examples/unity/EmailAuth.cs -->
```csharp
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using OpenPlay;
using OpenPlay.Session;
using OpenPlay.Support;

/// <summary>E-posta ve parola akışları. Parola SecretText ile taşınır; günlüğe ve ToString()'e girmez.</summary>
public static class EmailAuth
{
    public static Task<SessionState> Register(OpenPlayClient client, string email, string password, string displayName) =>
        client.Session.SignUpEmailAsync(new SignUpEmailInput { Email = email, Password = new SecretText(password), DisplayName = displayName });

    public static Task<SessionState> Login(OpenPlayClient client, string email, string password) =>
        client.Session.SignInEmailAsync(new SignInEmailInput { Email = email, Password = new SecretText(password) });

    /// <summary>Misafir ilerlemesini korur: aynı oyuncu, yeni oturum. Adres kayıtlıysa HTTP 409 gelir.</summary>
    public static async Task<SessionState> UpgradeGuest(OpenPlayClient client, string email, string password, string displayName)
    {
        try
        {
            return await client.Session.UpgradeGuestAsync(new UpgradeGuestInput { Email = email, Password = new SecretText(password), DisplayName = displayName });
        }
        catch (OpenPlayException ex) when (ex.Kind == ErrorKind.Http && ex.Status == 409)
        {
            throw new System.InvalidOperationException("Bu e-posta adresi başka bir hesapta kayıtlı.");
        }
    }

    public static Task RequestReset(OpenPlayClient client, string email) =>
        client.Session.RequestPasswordResetAsync(new PasswordResetRequestInput { Email = email });

    /// <summary>E-postadaki token ile yeni parola; başarıda tüm oturumlar kapanır.</summary>
    public static Task FinishReset(OpenPlayClient client, string token, string newPassword) =>
        client.Session.ResetPasswordAsync(new ResetPasswordInput { Token = new SecretText(token), NewPassword = new SecretText(newPassword) });

    /// <summary>Görünen ad kuralı: normalize sonrası 2..24 karakter, saatte 10 değişiklik.</summary>
    public static Task<SessionState> Rename(OpenPlayClient client, string displayName) =>
        client.Session.RenameAsync(displayName);
}
```

## Magic link

A magic link sends a single-use link to the email. The server endpoints exist, there is **no SDK helper yet**, and it is not documented as an in-game flow.

```http
POST /v1/auth/sign-in/magic-link
content-type: application/json
x-crx-env: <environment-id>
x-csrf-token: <csrf-token>

{"email":"player@example.com","name":"Alex"}
```

`200` (email existence does not leak); the token is only in the email, is valid for **5 minutes**, and is single-use. Limit: IP 5 / 60 s, address 5 / 15 min. The token is consumed with `GET /v1/auth/magic-link/verify?token=<token>`; on success `200`, the sign-in envelope, and session cookies are returned, and an invalid token gives `403`.

> [!WARNING]
> The `verify` endpoint also requires the `x-crx-env` header. In a browser, clicking the link in the email directly does not send this header and gets `401`. If you want to build a magic link flow, your game page must take the token and verify it with a request that carries the header; this flow is not covered by the SDKs or by this document.

# Verifying the player on your game server

Your game server wants to know which player an incoming request or connection belongs to. This page describes two paths: **verifying the access JWT that Open Play signed offline** (with the JWKS) and **verifying by asking Open Play about the session**. The comparison table at the end of the page tells you which path suits you.

> [!WARNING]
> **Today's SDK limit:** The TypeScript and Unity SDKs do **not expose** the access JWT to the application; the token exists only in the body of the raw REST sign-in response (`tokens.access_token`). A client that uses an SDK cannot give the JWT to your server. For SDK-based clients, **Path B** below (session verification) can be used. This gap is on record.

## Path A: verify the access JWT offline

### The token

The sign-in, sign-up, refresh, and OAuth callback responses return `tokens.access_token`: a compact JWT signed with **EdDSA (Ed25519)**, valid for **15 minutes**. The token is only in the body; it is not carried in a cookie. When it expires, `POST /v1/auth/refresh` gives a new one.

Header: `{"alg":"EdDSA","typ":"JWT","kid":"<key-id>"}`. Payload:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `sub` | Player ID (UUID) |
| `env` | Environment ID (UUID) |
| `sid` | Session ID (UUID) |
| `org` | Active organization (the field is absent if there is none) |
| `iat`, `exp` | Issue and expiry time: **Unix milliseconds** (not seconds) |

> [!WARNING]
> `iat` and `exp` are **milliseconds**, and the token has no `iss`/`aud`. Standard JWT libraries count `exp` in seconds; they mistake the millisecond value for a date in the far future and **accept an expired token.** If you use a library, have it verify the signature but do the `exp` check **yourself** (`exp > Date.now()`).

### Keys (JWKS)

```bash
curl -sS "$OPEN_PLAY_URL/v1/auth/jwks"
```

```json
{"keys":[{"kty":"OKP","crv":"Ed25519","kid":"<key-id>","use":"sig","alg":"EdDSA","x":"<base64url public key>"}]}
```

No credentials are needed. If the server has not published the signer, `404` is returned. Cache the response and refresh it only if the `kid` in the token is not in the list; do not fetch it on every request.

### Verification steps

1. Split the token into three parts; the `alg` value in the header must be `EdDSA`, otherwise reject it.
2. Find the key in the JWKS by `kid`; if it is missing, refresh the JWKS once.
3. Verify the signature over the `header.payload` text with Ed25519.
4. Check that `exp > now (ms)`.
5. **Compare the `env` value with your own environment ID.**

> [!WARNING]
> The tokens of all games on the same Open Play server are verified with the same JWKS. If you skip the `env` comparison, a player of another game appears valid on your server. This step is mandatory.

Node.js (only the built-in `node:crypto`, no dependencies):

<!-- snippet: examples/ts/verify-access-token.ts -->
```ts
import { createPublicKey, verify, type JsonWebKey } from "node:crypto";

/** Open Play erişim JWT'sinin (EdDSA) yük alanları. `iat` ve `exp` saniye değil, Unix MİLİSANİYEDİR. */
export interface AccessClaims {
  readonly sub: string; // oyuncu kimliği
  readonly env: string; // ortam kimliği
  readonly sid: string; // oturum kimliği
  readonly org?: string;
  readonly iat: number;
  readonly exp: number;
}

interface Jwks {
  readonly keys: ReadonlyArray<JsonWebKey & { readonly kid?: string }>;
}

function decode(part: string): Buffer {
  return Buffer.from(part, "base64url");
}

/**
 * Oyuncunun erişim JWT'sini çevrimdışı doğrular: imza, süre ve ortam eşleşmesi.
 * JWKS'i her istekte çekmeyin; `jwks` parametresini önbellekten verin (anahtar döndürülürse bilinmeyen `kid` gelince yenileyin).
 */
export function verifyAccessToken(token: string, jwks: Jwks, expectedEnvironmentId: string, nowMs: number = Date.now()): AccessClaims {
  const parts = token.split(".");
  const [header, payload, signature] = parts;
  if (parts.length !== 3 || header === undefined || payload === undefined || signature === undefined) {
    throw new Error("Biçimi bozuk JWT.");
  }
  const head = JSON.parse(decode(header).toString("utf8")) as { alg?: string; kid?: string };
  if (head.alg !== "EdDSA") throw new Error("Beklenmeyen algoritma.");
  const jwk = jwks.keys.find((key) => key.kid === head.kid);
  if (jwk === undefined) throw new Error("Bilinmeyen anahtar kimliği; JWKS'i yenileyin.");
  const publicKey = createPublicKey({ key: jwk, format: "jwk" });
  const signed = Buffer.from(`${header}.${payload}`, "utf8");
  if (!verify(null, signed, publicKey, decode(signature))) throw new Error("İmza doğrulanamadı.");

  const claims = JSON.parse(decode(payload).toString("utf8")) as AccessClaims;
  if (claims.exp <= nowMs) throw new Error("Belirtecin süresi dolmuş.");
  // JWKS tüm oyunlar için ortaktır: başka bir ortamın oyuncusunu kabul etmemek için `env` mutlaka karşılaştırılır.
  if (claims.env !== expectedEnvironmentId) throw new Error("Belirteç başka bir ortama ait.");
  return claims;
}

/** JWKS'i çeker (kimlik gerekmez). */
export async function fetchJwks(baseUrl: string): Promise<Jwks> {
  const response = await fetch(`${baseUrl}/v1/auth/jwks`);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`JWKS okunamadı: ${response.status}`);
  return (await response.json()) as Jwks;
}
```

### Limits

- **It does not see revocation.** Offline verification cannot know that the session was closed, revoked, or that the player was banned; the token looks valid until its `exp` (at most 15 minutes). For operations that must be revocation-aware, use Path B or accept this 15-minute window.
- **Key rotation.** A key change restarts the server; the new `kid` appears in the JWKS. Your cache must refresh when it sees an unknown `kid`.

## Path B: ask Open Play about the session

You resolve the player by forwarding the player's session token (the cookie value or the `Authorization: Bearer` token) and the environment ID to Open Play. In a browser game, requests that go to the game's origin already carry the session cookie (`__Secure-crx.session`); your server can read this value and forward it to Open Play as a Bearer.

```bash
curl -sS "$OPEN_PLAY_URL/v1/auth/session" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $PLAYER_SESSION_TOKEN" \
  -H "x-crx-env: $OPEN_PLAY_ENV"
```

`200` and `{"environment_id","player_id","session_id","is_guest","displayName","email",…}`. A revoked, expired, or banned session gives `401`. Because it is a `GET`, no CSRF token is needed.

> [!WARNING]
> The session token is an authority. Do not write it to plain-text logs, do not spread it to other services, and forward it only over TLS.

## Which path should I choose?

| | Path A: offline JWT | Path B: session query |
|---|---|---|
| Request to Open Play | None (the JWKS is cached) | One request per verification |
| Sees revocation/ban | No (up to 15 min delay) | Yes |
| Token source | Only the raw REST sign-in response; **the SDK does not provide it** | The cookie or Bearer session token; also works with an SDK-based client (the cookie in the browser) |
| Environment verification | You compare the `env` claim | The environment is checked against the `x-crx-env` in the request |
| When? | Very high request volume, a revocation delay is acceptable | The default recommendation; operations where player identity is critical |

For server-to-server calls, a **server key** is used instead of the player ([Concepts](../getting-started/concepts.md#api-keys-server-and-client)).

# Quickstart

In this guide you start from zero and do the following: create a game and a server key in the panel, make your first server call with `curl` and TypeScript, sign a player in as a guest from your browser game, and do the same from Unity. It takes about half an hour. Before you begin, skim the [Concepts](concepts.md) page.

**Prerequisites**

- A running Open Play server and an admin account that can sign in to the panel (super admin). For installation, see the [production deployment guide](../../../production-and-adapters.tr.md).
- The server's public HTTPS address. This page calls it `OPEN_PLAY_URL` (for your studio, the recommended `https://<slug>.open-play.tech/api` address; the older `https://<host>/api/<slug>` form, for example `https://open-play.b4klabs.dev/api/arenarace-stack`; the platform-level address `https://<host>/open-play` also continues to work).
- Node.js 22 or later and `curl`; `jq` and `openssl` for the raw REST examples.

## 1. Create a game and an environment

If the setup wizard has already created the first studio and game, skip this step.

1. In the central panel (`https://<host>/panel/`), open the **Studios** screen and create a studio with the **New studio** button.
2. Open the studio's detail page and choose **New game**; enter the game's name (and a short name if you like). The `dev` and `prod` environments are created automatically with the game.
3. In the **Active game and environment** selector in the top bar, choose the `dev` environment of the game you created. The rest of the panel shows the data of the selected environment.

Details: [Studios and games](../panel/tenancy.md).

## 2. Check the modules

On the **Game settings › Modules** tab, confirm that the `characters` module is **On** for the call in this guide. A disabled module returns `404` and `module_disabled`. Details: [Modules](../panel/game-settings-modules.md).

## 3. Create a server key

1. On the **Game settings › API keys** tab, choose **New API key**.
2. Give it a name (for example `Game server`), choose the type **Server key**, set the validity period, and write a short note for the audit log (optional; at most 500 characters).
3. Press Create. **Copy the secret value** on the card that opens **right away** and save it in your server's secret settings. You cannot see this value again.
4. The same card shows the **Ortam kimliği (environmentId)** (Environment ID) below the secret value. The SDK code in steps 6 and 7 asks for this value; it is not secret and you can always find it on the key's detail page.

The key has this format: `crx_live_<prefix>_<secret>`. On this page we will use it as environment variables:

```bash
export OPEN_PLAY_URL="https://<host>/api/<slug>"
export OPEN_PLAY_SERVER_KEY="crx_live_<prefix>_<secret>"
```

> [!WARNING]
> Do not write a real key in documents, repositories, chats, or logs. If you suspect it has leaked, rotate or delete the key in the panel.

## 4. Your first server call

With the server key, request the first page of characters in the environment. Because the environment comes from the key, no extra header is needed.

```bash
curl -sS "$OPEN_PLAY_URL/v1/characters?limit=1" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPEN_PLAY_SERVER_KEY"
```

If there are no characters yet, the expected response (`200`) is:

```json
{"items":[],"next_cursor":null}
```

The same call with the TypeScript SDK (for installation, see [TypeScript SDK](../sdk/typescript.md#installation)):

<!-- snippet: examples/ts/quickstart-server.ts -->
```ts
import { CrxClient, CrxError } from "@ferdiunal/open-play-ts";

/**
 * Sunucu anahtarıyla ilk çağrı: ortamdaki karakterlerin ilk sayfasını okur.
 * Bu kod yalnızca güvenilen arka uçta çalışır; sunucu anahtarını tarayıcıya göndermeyin.
 */
export async function listFirstCharacters(baseUrl: string, serverKey: string): Promise<number> {
  const client = new CrxClient({ baseUrl, apiKey: serverKey });
  try {
    const page = await client.listCharacters({ query: { limit: 1 } });
    return page.items.length;
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof CrxError) {
      // kind, status ve attempts güvenlidir; anahtar ve istek gövdesi hata nesnesine girmez.
      console.error("Open Play çağrısı başarısız:", error.kind, error.status);
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

// Kullanım (Node 22, ESM):
//   const count = await listFirstCharacters(process.env.OPEN_PLAY_URL!, process.env.OPEN_PLAY_SERVER_KEY!);
```

Possible problems:

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `401` | The key is wrong, deleted, expired, or the `Bearer` prefix is missing | Check the key and the header; create a new key if needed |
| `404`, body `{"code":"module_disabled",…}` | The `characters` module is disabled in this environment | Step 2 |
| `403` | The key type is insufficient for this operation (a client key) | Use a server key |
| Connection/TLS error | The address is wrong or is not HTTPS | Use the public HTTPS address; `http://localhost` is for local development only |

## 5. Bind your game's domain to the environment

The browser adds an `Origin` header to state-changing requests; the server checks that this origin belongs to that environment. If your game page's domain is not defined, player sign-in is rejected with `403`.

1. On the **Game settings › Environments and domains** tab, in the **Domains** field of the relevant environment, enter your game page's domain (one domain per line; for example `game.example.com`). Wildcards, IPs, and ports are not accepted.
2. Write a reason and press **Save domains**.

If you do not enter a domain, the server's global allowed list applies; that list is an operator setting. Details: [Architecture and hosting](architecture-and-hosting.md#domains-and-trusted-origins).

## 6. Your first player sign-in from the browser (guest)

Your game page calls the API either from the same origin (reverse proxy) or, if its domain is bound to an environment, directly at the Open Play address (CORS) (see [Architecture and hosting](architecture-and-hosting.md#calling-from-the-browser-direct-cors-or-reverse-proxy)). The code below restores a stored session; if there is none, it opens a guest session. `environmentId` is the environment's UUID; in the panel it is shown as **Ortam kimliği (environmentId)** (Environment ID) on the key's detail page (and on the card where you created the key, step 3).

<!-- snippet: examples/ts/quickstart-player.ts -->
```ts
import { createPlayerClient, type PlayerClient } from "@ferdiunal/open-play-ts";

/**
 * Tarayıcıdaki oyun sayfasından oyuncu oturumu açar: kayıtlı oturum varsa onu geri yükler,
 * yoksa misafir oturumu açar. `baseUrl` oyun sayfasıyla aynı origin'dir (aynı köken kuralı).
 */
export async function signInAsGuest(environmentId: string): Promise<PlayerClient> {
  const client = createPlayerClient({ baseUrl: window.location.origin, environmentId });

  // restore() yalnızca bakar; oturum yoksa misafir yaratmaz.
  const restored = await client.session.restore();
  const session = restored.status === "authenticated" ? restored : await client.session.signInGuest();

  console.log("oyuncu:", session.playerId, "misafir mi:", session.isGuest, "ad:", session.displayName);
  return client;
}
```

The SDK generates and stores the guest secret for you, gets the CSRF token, and manages the cookies. When you refresh the page in the same browser, `restore()` brings back the same player.

### The same with raw REST

The script below shows what the SDK does behind the scenes (HTTPS is required; the server writes a `Secure` cookie):

```bash
export OPEN_PLAY_ENV="<environment-id-uuid>"
JAR="$(mktemp)"

# 1) Get a CSRF token (it is also written to the cookie jar)
TOKEN="$(curl -sS -c "$JAR" "$OPEN_PLAY_URL/v1/auth/csrf" | jq -r .token)"

# 2) A 32-byte random guest secret: 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters. As long as you keep it, it returns to the same player.
BOOTSTRAP="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"

# 3) Guest sign-in
curl -sS -b "$JAR" -c "$JAR" -X POST "$OPEN_PLAY_URL/v1/auth/sign-in/guest" \
  -H "content-type: application/json" \
  -H "x-crx-env: $OPEN_PLAY_ENV" \
  -H "x-csrf-token: $TOKEN" \
  -d "{\"bootstrap_token\":\"$BOOTSTRAP\"}"
```

Response (`200`; `is_new_player` is `true` the first time):

```json
{
  "player": { "id": "0192…", "display_name": "Guest", "email": null, "email_verified": false, "is_guest": true, "image": null, "created_at": "2026-11-03T10:00:00+00:00" },
  "tokens": { "session_token": "<64 hex>", "refresh_token": "<64 hex>", "access_token": "<jwt>", "expires_in_secs": 900 },
  "is_new_player": true
}
```

The cookie jar now holds `__Secure-crx.session` and `__Secure-crx.refresh`. Verify the session:

```bash
curl -sS -b "$JAR" "$OPEN_PLAY_URL/v1/auth/session" -H "x-crx-env: $OPEN_PLAY_ENV"
```

On every state-changing request the CSRF token is **single-use**: the response's `x-csrf-token` header carries the next one. Details: [Sessions and CSRF](../auth/sessions-and-csrf.md), [Guest sign-in](../auth/guest.md).

## 7. Your first player sign-in from Unity

After installing the Unity package ([Unity SDK](../sdk/unity.md#installation)), add the component below to a `GameObject`. Fill in the `baseUrl` and `environmentId` fields (`environmentId`: the environment ID from step 3). In a WebGL build, `baseUrl` must be the same origin as the page.

<!-- snippet: examples/unity/QuickstartGuest.cs -->
```csharp
using System;
using OpenPlay;
using OpenPlay.Session;
using OpenPlay.Unity;
using UnityEngine;

/// <summary>
/// Misafir girişi: kayıtlı oturum varsa geri yükler, yoksa misafir oturumu açar.
/// Bir GameObject'e ekleyin; baseUrl ve environmentId alanlarını doldurun.
/// </summary>
public sealed class QuickstartGuest : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private string baseUrl = "https://oyun.example.com";   // WebGL'de sayfayla aynı origin olmalı
    [SerializeField] private string environmentId = "";                      // ortam kimliği (UUID)
    [SerializeField] private bool allowInsecureTransport = false;            // yalnız http://localhost için

    private OpenPlayClient _client;

    private async void Start()
    {
        try
        {
            var options = OpenPlayUnity.Options(new Uri(baseUrl), Guid.Parse(environmentId));
            options.AllowInsecureTransport = allowInsecureTransport;
            _client = OpenPlayClient.Create(options);

            var state = await _client.Session.RestoreAsync();   // oturum yoksa misafir YARATMAZ
            if (state.Status != SessionStatus.Authenticated) state = await _client.Session.SignInGuestAsync();

            if (state.Status == SessionStatus.Banned)
            {
                Debug.Log("Hesap askıya alındı."); // terminal durum; yalnız LogoutAsync ile çıkılır
                return;
            }
            Debug.Log("hazır: oyuncu=" + state.PlayerId + " misafir=" + state.IsGuest);
        }
        catch (OpenPlayException ex)
        {
            Debug.LogError("Open Play hatası: " + ex.Kind + " (" + ex.Code + ")");
        }
    }

    private async void OnDestroy()
    {
        if (_client != null) await _client.DisposeAsync();
    }
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> The Unity SDK is verified only with Unity 6000.6.7f1. For details, see [Unity SDK](../sdk/unity.md#verified-platforms).

